---
title: Reaping & Retention (Advanced)
id: sandbox-reaping
order: 14
description: "Schedule the sweep that finalizes detached runs, prunes journals, and reclaims sandboxes — and understand why a durable run that nothing reaps never lands its transcript."
keywords:
  - reapDetachedRuns
  - probeRunExit
  - pruneJournals
  - reclaimSandbox
  - sandboxReclaimer
  - SandboxReclaimFailedError
  - detachedRunTtlMs
  - retention
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" makes a disconnect survivable: the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. That is only half a lifecycle. Something has to be the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"end"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of a run\nthat no viewer ever came back for."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That something is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":", and it is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"function, not a scheduler"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"read-this-first-nothing-schedules-it-for-you","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read this first: nothing schedules it for you"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An application that wires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" and never schedules\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" is not running a reduced version of this feature. It is\nrunning a broken one, in three distinct ways at once:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No detached run's delivery log is ever closed."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A detached run's log is\ndeliberately left OPEN and un-terminalized so a takeover can continue it. If\nnothing ever terminalizes it, every client that attaches parks forever waiting\nfor an event that will not arrive."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" is enforced by nothing."}]},{"type":"text","value":" It is not a timer. It is a\ncutoff the sweep compares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" against, and it exists only as an\nargument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" — there is no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" equivalent. With no\nsweep, it is a number nobody ever reads and an abandoned agent burns tokens\nuntil something else kills it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandboxes bill indefinitely."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Detach-on-disconnect exists precisely so the\nsandbox is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" destroyed. Reclaiming it is the sweep's job."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no default cron, no background timer, and no warning at setup — a timer\ninside a library is wrong on every serverless platform the sandbox packages\ntarget. So treat scheduling as a hard requirement of wiring "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":", on the\nsame footing as passing a real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LockStore"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-the-reaper-is-correctness-not-just-cost","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why the reaper is correctness, not just cost"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The cost story is the obvious one. The correctness story is the reason\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'finalized'"},{"type":"text","value":" exists as an outcome at all."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" saves a run's transcript in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A run that\ncompletes while detached never reaches anyone's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":": the host that would\nhave run it is the host that left when the client disconnected. The agent\nfinished, its bytes are in the in-sandbox journal, the delivery log is frozen at\nthe last chunk that was actually delivered, and the message store has nothing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing recovers that on its own. A later takeover would — but a takeover only\nhappens if a user comes back, and the entire premise of a detached run is that\nthey might not. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" is the actor that drives such a run through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"'s normal middleware path so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" fires and the transcript lands.\nThat is what "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'finalized'"},{"type":"text","value":" means, and it is why a reaper you never schedule\nsilently loses completed work rather than merely wasting money."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reapdetachedruns","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { reapDetachedRuns } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One sweep, in order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ask "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs: 0 })"},{"type":"text","value":" — "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"once"}]},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ttlMs: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" is\n"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every"}]},{"type":"text","value":" detached run, because that is the candidate set for finalization: a\nrun that hit its sentinel one second after the viewer left has an unsaved\ntranscript and must not wait out the TTL. Expiry is then classified\nin-process against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"now - detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":", inclusively, so the store is not\nasked twice to compute a subset."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each run in the batch (capped by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxRuns"},{"type":"text","value":", default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"25"},{"type":"text","value":"): classify\nexpiry first, otherwise probe, and only then claim, quiesce, drive, and\nreclaim."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":". A backend that omits it answers\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ considered: 0 }"},{"type":"text","value":" and one log line — it cannot be reaped at all."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The function "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"never rejects"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It runs from a cron, an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"},{"type":"text","value":", or a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitUntil"},{"type":"text","value":", where nobody is there to catch it, so every per-run failure is\nlogged and folded into the returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapResult"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcomes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapResult.runs"},{"type":"text","value":" is one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapRunEntry"},{"type":"text","value":" per run, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapResult.outcomes"},{"type":"text","value":" counts\nthem by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapRunOutcome"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finalized"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The probe saw the exit sentinel, the run was driven to terminal, the transcript is saved. The happy path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expired"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Past "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":". Cancelled first (so the teardown is an explicit cancel that destroys the sandbox), then driven to terminal. The probe is skipped — the outcome is terminal either way. Reported even when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runBudgetMs"},{"type":"text","value":" is what ended the drive: on this path that is the mechanism, not an anomaly. The run's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" tells the two apart — an agent that had already finished replays to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":", one still producing when the budget fired is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"producing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Still working. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" was never entered: nothing appended, no record written, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" not called, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" untouched."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The probe could not answer. Left exactly as untouched as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"producing"},{"type":"text","value":", but an operator should see it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"budget-exceeded"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anomaly, and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"finalization only"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A run the journal already said was finished outran "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runBudgetMs"},{"type":"text","value":". The record "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" terminal and the log "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" closed, so this is a diagnostic, not a leak. An expired run that outran its budget reports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expired"},{"type":"text","value":" instead. The entry also carries "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"terminalizedAnyway"},{"type":"text","value":", which is set "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"if and only if"}]},{"type":"text","value":" this anomaly happened — so it survives even when a subsequent failed reclaim overwrites the outcome."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"not-claimed"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Another host holds the claim, or took it mid-drive. Normal — a real viewer attaching mid-sweep is exactly this."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim-failed"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run reached terminal and its transcript "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" saved — only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions.reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":" threw, so the sandbox is still up. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not retryable by the sweep"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the record is terminal by now, so it has already left "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" for good, and nothing will sweep it again. This is the outcome that says the cost leak the reaper exists to stop is still leaking; the entry's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" is the only notice you get. The shipped "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rejects"}]},{"type":"text","value":" on its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy-failed"},{"type":"text","value":" arm precisely so this is reachable without a custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":". Overwrites "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"budget-exceeded"},{"type":"text","value":" when both happened — the leak is what needs acting on, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"terminalizedAnyway"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the other half."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Something threw. Logged, counted, and the sweep continued."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is deliberately no \"still running\" outcome distinct from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"producing"},{"type":"text","value":", and\nno outcome meaning \"we drove it and it turned out not to be finished\" — see\nbelow for why that state is unreachable by construction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"it-never-drives-a-run-to-find-out-whether-it-finished","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It never drives a run to find out whether it finished"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the one rule that shapes the whole module, and it is worth understanding\nbefore you wire anything, because the obvious alternative is worse than it looks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The tempting design is: hand the run to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" under a short budget and\nsee whether it terminalizes. That does not work, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" is total\nby construction — it "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"always"}]},{"type":"text","value":" writes a terminal status and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"always"}]},{"type":"text","value":" calls\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability.close()"},{"type":"text","value":", on every path. Against a run that has "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" finished, all\nthree producer shapes are destructive:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The producer's reaction to the budget signal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stored status"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ignores it and keeps producing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"called"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns on abort (the realistic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"drive"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"called"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throws an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"called"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The middle row used to read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":", and that was the fatal one: a\nsignal-aware producer — the shape a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"well-behaved"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"drive"},{"type":"text","value":" actually has — exits\nits loop normally, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" checked its abort signal only per chunk, so\na healthy mid-flight run was recorded as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'completed'"},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishedAt"},{"type":"text","value":". That\ngap is fixed; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" re-checks the signal after the loop, so an aborted\ndrive is never recorded as completed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rule is unchanged, though, because the status was never the whole harm: every\nrow above writes a terminal record and closes a log that was deliberately left\nopen, ending every attached client's stream — and a terminal record drops out of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"forever"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so TTL expiry can never reclaim that run's sandbox.\nA cost leak with no recovery path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So finished-ness is learned "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"out of band"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from the in-sandbox journal, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pipeToRunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" is entered only for a run already known to have finished or one\nwhose TTL has expired (terminal either way). On the finalization path "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runBudgetMs"},{"type":"text","value":"\ntherefore degrades from a load-bearing mechanism into a safety net whose expiry is\na genuine anomaly. On the expiry path it stays load-bearing: nothing polls the\ncancel the reaper records, so the budget is what stops an expired run whose agent\nis still producing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sweep also "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"never clears "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"}]},{"type":"text","value":". That field is what the reaper\nselects on and the evidence its TTL accounting used. Clearing it would reset the\nTTL on every sweep and a detached run would never expire. (The takeover path\nclears it, because there a real viewer genuinely stopped the clock.)"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"proberunexit-and-why-hasfinished-is-injected","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"probeRunExit"},{"type":"text","value":", and why "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasFinished"},{"type":"text","value":" is injected"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions.hasFinished"},{"type":"text","value":" is a required option you supply. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"probeRunExit"},{"type":"text","value":" is the\nshipped implementation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { probeRunExit } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It reads the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tail"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of the run's journal (4 KB by default,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_EXIT_PROBE_BYTES"},{"type":"text","value":") and answers whether the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{\"__exit\":N}"},{"type":"text","value":" sentinel is\nthere. Read-only: no append, no record write, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":". Its three-armed\nanswer — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finished"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"producing"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" — is not a boolean on purpose, so a\nprovider "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" that rejected can never be mistaken for \"the agent exited\". Any\nfailure answers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":"; an empty tail answers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"producing"},{"type":"text","value":", the fail-safe\ndirection (a journal that does not exist yet is indistinguishable from one with\nno sentinel, and both mean "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"do not touch this run"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is injected rather than resolved inside the reaper for two reasons, both\nstructural:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The delivery log cannot answer the question."}]},{"type":"text","value":" After a detach nothing appends\nto it — the host that would have appended is the host that left — so the log is\nfrozen at the last delivered chunk while the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" keeps growing. The log\ncan only ever say \"no news\"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only your application can resolve a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxHandle"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxInstanceStore"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":" with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":" (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#the-limitation-there-is-no-instance-store-list","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the named limitation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"), and\nmapping a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord.sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":" to a live handle is application knowledge."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions.reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":" is injected for the same reason, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer"},{"type":"text","value":" is its ready-made implementation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-wire-the-sweep-once","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server: wire the sweep once"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Everything the sweep needs is what your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":" route already has, plus a way to\nresolve a sandbox handle from a key. Put it in one module and let each schedule\ncall it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  probeRunExit,\n  reapDetachedRuns,\n  sandboxReclaimer,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { ReapResult, RunExitProbe } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your distributed LockStore, the same one `withSandbox` gets.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your persistence — the SAME RunStore the chat routes use.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result and the `SandboxInstanceStore` you passed to\n// `withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })`.\nimport { instances, sandbox } from './sandbox'\n// The same `drive` the attach route passes to `sandboxRunDriver` — a function of\n// `{ runId, threadId, signal }` that runs `chat()` with `durability.attach: true`.\n// See ./takeover, \"Server: take the run over\".\nimport { driveRun } from './drive-run'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n// The per-run log factory, and it MUST resolve the same log the producing route\n// wrote — otherwise the sweep terminalizes an empty log while the real one stays\n// open. A cron has no incoming request, so synthesize one naming the run.\n//\n// `?runId` is the form to use here: `durableStream` resolves a run from the\n// `X-Run-Id` header first, then `?runId` (the same precedence core's own\n// `memoryStream` uses), but a synthesized `Request` has no reason to carry a\n// header when a query param is just as easy to set — either addresses the\n// same `agent-runs/<runId>` the chat routes' `durableStream(request,\n// durableOptions)` addresses. No resume offset is set, because the reaper is\n// a producer, not a replaying client.\n//\n// The same backend and options the chat routes use — see ../resumable-streams/\n// advanced for the full option set. `durableStream` talks to it over HTTP, so a\n// synthesized request works exactly like a real one: the run's state lives in\n// the backend, not in this process.\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}\n\nfunction durabilityFor(runId: string) {\n  const url = new URL('https://reaper.internal/')\n  url.searchParams.set('runId', runId)\n  return durableStream(new Request(url), durableOptions)\n}\n\n// Only the application can map a recorded `sandboxKey` back to a live handle:\n// the instance store answers `get(key)` but never enumerates, and resolving the\n// provider sandbox id it holds is your provider's `resume`. Anything this cannot\n// answer must be `unknown`, never `finished`.\nasync function hasFinished(record: RunRecord): Promise<RunExitProbe> {\n  if (record.sandboxKey === undefined) return { state: 'unknown' }\n  try {\n    const instance = await instances.get(record.sandboxKey)\n    if (instance === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    const handle = await sandbox.provider.resume({\n      id: instance.providerSandboxId,\n    })\n    if (handle === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    return await probeRunExit({ handle, runId: record.runId })\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { state: 'unknown', error }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function sweepDetachedRuns(): Promise<ReapResult> {\n  return reapDetachedRuns({\n    runs,\n    locks,\n    durability: durabilityFor,\n    hasFinished,\n    drive: driveRun,\n    now: Date.now(),\n    // The only place this TTL is configured — there is no `withSandbox`\n    // equivalent. In milliseconds.\n    detachedRunTtlMs: 30 * 60 * 1000,\n    // Sequential by design — each run costs a lock, a provider round-trip, and a\n    // full replay. Keep the batch inside your platform's invocation budget.\n    maxRuns: 25,\n    reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({\n      provider: sandbox.provider,\n      instances,\n    }),\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves rather than rejects, so log the summary and let the\nschedule keep its cadence:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// Plain Node, no platform cron. One in flight at a time: a sweep that overruns\n// its interval must not be started twice, or two invocations race for the same\n// claims and every second one reports `not-claimed`.\nlet inFlight = false\n\nasync function tick(): Promise<void> {\n  if (inFlight) return\n  inFlight = true\n  try {\n    const result: ReapResult = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n    console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    for (const run of result.runs) {\n      if (run.outcome === 'failed' || run.outcome === 'unknown') {\n        console.warn('reap needs attention', run.runId, run.outcome, run.error)\n      }\n    }\n  } finally {\n    inFlight = false\n  }\n}\n\nsetInterval(() => void tick(), 60_000)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"vercel-cron","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel Cron"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A cron route is a plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":". Guard it — it drives runs and destroys sandboxes,\nso it must not be publicly callable."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const secret = process.env.CRON_SECRET\n  if (\n    secret === undefined ||\n    request.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${secret}`\n  ) {\n    return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n  return Response.json({\n    considered: result.considered,\n    probed: result.probed,\n    outcomes: result.outcomes,\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Register it in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vercel.json"},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"path"},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"schedule"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*/5 * * * *"},{"type":"text","value":" is a\nreasonable start — see "},{"type":"link","href":"#sizing-detachedrunttl-and-the-sweep-interval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sizing"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cloudflare-a-durable-object-alarm","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare: a Durable Object "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A DO alarm is the natural scheduler on Workers: it is single-instance, so the\n\"one sweep in flight\" guard is free, and it re-arms itself."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// The slice of `DurableObjectState` this needs. In a real Worker this is\n// `state.storage` from your Workers types.\ninterface AlarmStorage {\n  setAlarm: (scheduledTime: number) => Promise<void>\n}\n\nexport class RunReaper {\n  constructor(private readonly storage: AlarmStorage) {}\n\n  async alarm(): Promise<void> {\n    try {\n      const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n      console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    } finally {\n      // Re-arm in `finally`. An alarm that throws without rescheduling stops\n      // reaping forever, which is exactly the failure mode this page is about.\n      await this.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 60_000)\n    }\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cloudflare-s-stall-watchdog-is-not-a-reaper","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare's stall watchdog is not a reaper"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ stallTimeoutMs })"},{"type":"text","value":" sets an app-wide policy for\nboth "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"do-drives"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colocated"},{"type":"text","value":" modes. Omit it for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"300000"},{"type":"text","value":" ms default, pass\na positive safe-integer millisecond value to customize it, or pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" to\ndisable it. Its alarm checks about every 30 seconds."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persisted events and authenticated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/_bridge"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tool-exec"},{"type":"text","value":" callback arrival\nand completion refresh the run record's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":"; unknown-run and\nunauthorized requests do not. Silent native operations therefore need a timeout\nlonger than their longest expected quiet period. In-flight authenticated\ncallbacks are protected. A callback that never completes can leave an orphaned\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"running"},{"type":"text","value":" record. Disabling the watchdog with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" can do the same."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The watchdog atomically marks a stale log "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":", but may not kill the\nunderlying process and never probes a journal or reclaims a sandbox. You must\nstill schedule "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sweepDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":"; see the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./cloudflare#stall-watchdog","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare stall-watchdog details"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prunejournals-bounding-the-journal-directory","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":": bounding the journal directory"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reaper terminalizes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does not tidy the\njournal directory "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"inside"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a sandbox that is still alive — a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepAlive"},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox\nserving many turns accumulates journals for every run whose exit sentinel nobody\nwas there to observe."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { pruneJournals } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { handleForSandbox } from './sandbox-handles'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function sweepJournals(sandboxKey: string) {\n  const result = await pruneJournals({\n    handle: await handleForSandbox(sandboxKey),\n    // Only `get` is used: the sweep asks about the runIds it found on disk and\n    // never enumerates the store, so no optional `RunStore` method is needed.\n    runs,\n  })\n  if (result.ageGate === 'unavailable') {\n    console.warn('journal sweep could not age-gate; kept every orphan')\n  }\n  return result\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It fails closed everywhere."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The journal is the only copy of the bytes a\nsuccessor host needs to replay a run a dead host abandoned, so the decision\nprocedure is not \"delete unless there is a reason to keep\" — it is the opposite,\nand every arm that is not a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"proven"}]},{"type":"text","value":"-safe deletion keeps:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What the store says"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isTerminalRunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"delete"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The delivery log, not the journal, is the record a late takeover aligns against. A non-zero exit is terminal too."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-terminal — including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'interrupted'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interrupt is a human-in-the-loop "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pause"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; interrupt-resume continues from that journal."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing (unknown runId)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep until "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orphanTtlMs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reader "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"creates"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the journal before the record exists, so \"unknown runId\" is the normal state of a run that started moments ago."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lookup threw"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An unanswered question is not a licence to delete."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The filename did not decode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncated name decodes to a plausible but "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"wrong"}]},{"type":"text","value":" runId, so asking the store would answer about some other — possibly live — run."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mtime age gate is unavailable"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep every age-gated entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cannot age-gate ⇒ cannot expire. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unavailable"},{"type":"text","value":" is a first-class result, never an empty listing."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That last row is the trap the module exists to not fall into. BusyBox "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"find"},{"type":"text","value":"\nprints its \"unrecognized option\" diagnostic to stderr and exits "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1 with empty\nstdout"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; code that reads that as \"no file is newer than the cutoff\" and\nconcludes \"therefore every file is old\" deletes the whole directory, live runs\nincluded. The mtime listing carries a self-witness line and reports\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unavailable"},{"type":"text","value":" rather than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":" honors that as \"I keep\"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deletions per sweep are capped by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDeletes"},{"type":"text","value":" (default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_MAX_DELETES"},{"type":"text","value":",\n200); the remainder is reported as kept with reason "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"max-deletes"},{"type":"text","value":" and picked up\nnext time. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orphanTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_ORPHAN_TTL_MS"},{"type":"text","value":" (one hour) —\nthree orders of magnitude of headroom on the create-then-record race, because the\ncost of too long is bytes and the cost of too short is a destroyed live run.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":" never rejects either; failures land in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PruneJournalsResult.failures"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reclaimsandbox-and-sandboxreclaimer","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { reclaimSandbox, sandboxReclaimer } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox(record, { provider, instances })"},{"type":"text","value":" destroys the sandbox a terminal\nrun was bound to, using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord.sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":" — recorded by the detach path at\nthe moment it still knew the compound key, because the reaper has none of the\ninputs ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", workspace hash, tenant, reuse strategy) needed to re-derive\nit. It answers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroyed'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroy-failed'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'no-sandbox-key'"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'not-found'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'provider-mismatch'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two orderings are load-bearing:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The provider check comes first"}]},{"type":"text","value":", before either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":". A\nmulti-provider app would otherwise hand a Docker container id to Daytona's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy"},{"type":"text","value":", which at best errors and at worst matches an unrelated sandbox in\nthe other provider's id namespace. A mismatch therefore touches "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nothing"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\nincluding the instance record, which the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"right"}]},{"type":"text","value":" provider still needs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":" regardless of whether "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy"},{"type":"text","value":"\nthrew."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The provider sandbox may already be gone (idle-reclaimed, region\nwiped, container pruned). Keeping an instance record that points at nothing\nguarantees a failed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resume"},{"type":"text","value":" on the thread's next turn — a broken user\nexperience — whereas an orphaned provider sandbox is a bounded cost the\nprovider itself reclaims. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":" is therefore unconditional, but the\n"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"outcome"}]},{"type":"text","value":" must not claim success when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroy"},{"type":"text","value":" threw: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroy-failed'"},{"type":"text","value":" is\nreported instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroyed'"},{"type":"text","value":", because the instance record is gone\neither way and an operator needs to tell \"torn down cleanly\" apart from\n\"possibly still billing, and now unreachable from here since\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxInstanceStore"},{"type":"text","value":" has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":"\". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer"},{"type":"text","value":" logs\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroy-failed'"},{"type":"text","value":" above debug level for exactly that reason — every other\noutcome is bookkeeping an operator never needs to see."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer(options)"},{"type":"text","value":" is the same thing adapted to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions.reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":":\nit logs the outcome and resolves — "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"except on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'destroy-failed'"},{"type":"text","value":", where it\nrejects"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxReclaimFailedError"},{"type":"text","value":". The reaper calls it "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"only"}]},{"type":"text","value":" once\nthe record actually reached a terminal status, and with the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"originally listed"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nrecord rather than the one the drive returned — a failed terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":"\nyields a locally rebuilt record with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":", which would answer\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'no-sandbox-key'"},{"type":"text","value":" and leak the sandbox silently on exactly the path where\nsomething already went wrong."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That rejection is deliberate, and it is what makes the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim-failed"},{"type":"text","value":" outcome\nreachable at all. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions.reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(record) => Promise<void>"},{"type":"text","value":", so a\nrejection is the sweep's only channel for \"the sandbox was NOT reclaimed\":"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire the reclaimer as shown above ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({ provider, instances })"},{"type":"text","value":"), then inspect the summary the sweep hands back:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { SandboxReclaimFailedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nexport function alertOnLeakedSandboxes(\n  result: ReapResult,\n  alert: (message: string, detail: Record<string, unknown>) => void,\n): void {\n  // Watch this counter — it is the leak alarm.\n  if (result.outcomes['reclaim-failed'] === 0) return\n\n  for (const run of result.runs) {\n    if (run.outcome !== 'reclaim-failed') continue\n    // The transcript IS saved and the record IS terminal; only the teardown\n    // failed. `status`/`exitCode` are reported exactly as `finalized` reports\n    // them, which is what distinguishes this from a `failed` sweep.\n    const leakedKey =\n      run.error instanceof SandboxReclaimFailedError\n        ? run.error.sandboxKey\n        : undefined\n\n    alert('sandbox may still be billing', {\n      runId: run.runId,\n      status: run.status,\n      sandboxKey: leakedKey,\n      // Present ONLY if the drive also outran `runBudgetMs`. `reclaim-failed`\n      // overwrites the `budget-exceeded` outcome, so this field is what keeps\n      // that second diagnostic on the entry.\n      budgetAnomaly: run.terminalizedAnyway !== undefined,\n    })\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim-failed"},{"type":"text","value":" run has already left "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" for good, so no later\nsweep retries it — that entry is the only notice you will get. A custom\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaim"},{"type":"text","value":" should follow the same convention: reject when the sandbox was not\ntorn down, resolve when there was nothing to tear down."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sizing-detachedrunttlms-and-the-sweep-interval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sizing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" and the sweep interval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" is a wall-clock cap, in milliseconds, on a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"running agent\nwith nobody watching"}]},{"type":"text","value":". There is no default and no string parsing: it is a\nrequired, plain number on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReapOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and deliberately lives only there —\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot enforce a TTL itself, since "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" runs from a\ncron with no chat request in flight and has no capability bus to read it\nfrom. Passing it directly to the sweep is what keeps it a single source of\ntruth instead of two settings that could silently disagree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Size it from your agent's honest p99 task duration, not from user patience: too\nshort and you cancel real work a user was going to come back to; too long and an\nabandoned run bills for that long. If a coding agent legitimately runs 20\nminutes, 30 minutes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"30 * 60 * 1000"},{"type":"text","value":") is tight and an hour is defensible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then keep the sweep interval well "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"under"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the TTL. The interval bounds how\nlong "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"past"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the TTL an expired run survives, and it also bounds how long a run\nthat finished while detached waits before its transcript lands. A one-minute\nalarm against a 30-minute TTL costs one cheap store query per minute and makes\nboth windows negligible. Sweeping every 30 minutes against a 30-minute TTL means\nan expiry can be an hour late."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 25) and the sequential per-run loop exist so one invocation\ncannot outrun a Worker's CPU budget or a Lambda timeout and be killed mid-drive.\nIf a backlog exceeds the batch, the next tick takes the next batch — reduce the\ninterval rather than raising the cap."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retention-three-clocks-and-only-one-of-them-is-ours","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retention: three clocks, and only one of them is ours"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reaper closes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" garbage-collect\nyour stored data, and the split is deliberate:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event-log retention is your durability backend's job"}]},{"type":"text","value":", inside whatever\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" you wired. The framework never deletes from it, because it\ndoes not own the storage — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" evicts completed runs after a grace\nwindow, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" retains per its backend's own policy, and a custom\nadapter retains per yours. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"30 days is a reasonable default"}]},{"type":"text","value":": long enough\nthat a user who reopens a thread the next morning still gets an exact replay of\nthe run, short enough that a chatty deployment does not accumulate raw chunk\nlogs forever."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Message retention is the message store's job"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and it must "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"outlive"}]},{"type":"text","value":" event\nretention. The transcript is the durable artifact; the event log is a delivery\ndetail. Once a run's events age out, the thread must still render — see the\nclient half below."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Journals are bounded inside the sandbox"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":", and vanish with\nthe sandbox when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" destroys it."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ordering is what matters: events may age out first, messages must not."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-tolerate-an-aged-out-event-log","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client: tolerate an aged-out event log"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The usual case needs nothing. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: true"},{"type":"text","value":" fetches the\ntranscript on mount and only then tails whatever run is still generating, so a\nrun whose event log is gone simply paints from messages."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Thread({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  // On mount this GETs the transcript from the message store, then tails an\n  // `activeRun` if `reconstructChat` reports one. An aged-out event log means\n  // the tail yields nothing — the transcript is already painted, so the thread\n  // still renders correctly. This is why message retention must outlive event\n  // retention.\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{messages.length} messages</p>\n      <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Continue')}>Continue</button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you drive the rejoin yourself, make the fallback explicit: join the run, and\nif it yields nothing, fall back to the stored transcript rather than showing an\nempty thread."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ThreadView({ threadId, runId }: {\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState('joining')\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function join(): Promise<void> {\n      let received = 0\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        received += 1\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n      // Zero chunks from a run the server knew about means its delivery log\n      // aged out (or was pruned). The transcript is still authoritative.\n      if (received === 0) setStatus('replaced-by-transcript')\n      else setStatus('joined')\n    }\n\n    void join().catch(() => setStatus('replaced-by-transcript'))\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [runId])\n\n  // Reconstructed from the message store — always render this, and let the\n  // joined events refine it. Never gate the thread on the event log existing.\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>\n        {status}: {chunks.length} live events, thread {threadId}\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same rule applies server-side: a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" that only replays the log will serve\nan empty stream for an aged-out run. Route reconstruct-from-messages first and\nresume second, as "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence Overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-limitation-there-is-no-instance-store-list","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The limitation: there is no instance-store "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxInstanceStore"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":", with no enumeration, and\nthat is a deliberate refusal: adding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":" would force every backend and the\nconformance suite to grow an enumeration for one hypothetical caller."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The consequence is real and documented rather than hidden. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sandbox whose run\nrecord was deleted before any sweep saw it is unreachable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Nothing can name its\nkey, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" can never be called for it, and it survives until the\nprovider's own idle reclamation takes it. Two things keep that rare: prune run\nrecords only after their runs are terminal "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"and"}]},{"type":"text","value":" their sandboxes reclaimed, and\nset a provider-side idle timeout as the backstop."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"see-also","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See also"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./durable-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Runs Explained"}]},{"type":"text","value":": where this sweep fits in the whole\npicture, in plain language and with no code"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": how a run becomes detached in the first\nplace, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxRunDriver"},{"type":"text","value":", and the single-writer fencing the sweep reuses"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the journal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"probeRunExit"},{"type":"text","value":" reads and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/store-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":":\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":", and the run fields a reapable backend must round-trip"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./durability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandbox Instance Durability"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the instance store\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" deletes from"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"read-this-first-nothing-schedules-it-for-you","text":"Read this first: nothing schedules it for you","level":2},{"id":"why-the-reaper-is-correctness-not-just-cost","text":"Why the reaper is correctness, not just cost","level":2},{"id":"reapdetachedruns","text":"reapDetachedRuns","level":2},{"id":"outcomes","text":"Outcomes","level":3},{"id":"it-never-drives-a-run-to-find-out-whether-it-finished","text":"It never drives a run to find out whether it finished","level":3},{"id":"proberunexit-and-why-hasfinished-is-injected","text":"probeRunExit, and why hasFinished is injected","level":2},{"id":"server-wire-the-sweep-once","text":"Server: wire the sweep once","level":2},{"id":"vercel-cron","text":"Vercel Cron","level":3},{"id":"cloudflare-a-durable-object-alarm","text":"Cloudflare: a Durable Object alarm()","level":3},{"id":"cloudflare-s-stall-watchdog-is-not-a-reaper","text":"Cloudflare's stall watchdog is not a reaper","level":3},{"id":"prunejournals-bounding-the-journal-directory","text":"pruneJournals: bounding the journal directory","level":2},{"id":"reclaimsandbox-and-sandboxreclaimer","text":"reclaimSandbox and sandboxReclaimer","level":2},{"id":"sizing-detachedrunttlms-and-the-sweep-interval","text":"Sizing detachedRunTtlMs and the sweep interval","level":2},{"id":"retention-three-clocks-and-only-one-of-them-is-ours","text":"Retention: three clocks, and only one of them is ours","level":2},{"id":"client-tolerate-an-aged-out-event-log","text":"Client: tolerate an aged-out event log","level":2},{"id":"the-limitation-there-is-no-instance-store-list","text":"The limitation: there is no instance-store list","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover) makes a disconnect survivable: the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. That is only half a lifecycle. Something has to be the *end* of a run\nthat no viewer ever came back for.\n\nThat something is `reapDetachedRuns`, and it is a **function, not a scheduler**.\n\n## Read this first: nothing schedules it for you\n\nAn application that wires `runs` + `durability` and never schedules\n`reapDetachedRuns` is not running a reduced version of this feature. It is\nrunning a broken one, in three distinct ways at once:\n\n- **No detached run's delivery log is ever closed.** A detached run's log is\n  deliberately left OPEN and un-terminalized so a takeover can continue it. If\n  nothing ever terminalizes it, every client that attaches parks forever waiting\n  for an event that will not arrive.\n- **`detachedRunTtlMs` is enforced by nothing.** It is not a timer. It is a\n  cutoff the sweep compares `detachedSince` against, and it exists only as an\n  argument to `reapDetachedRuns` — there is no `withSandbox` equivalent. With no\n  sweep, it is a number nobody ever reads and an abandoned agent burns tokens\n  until something else kills it.\n- **Sandboxes bill indefinitely.** Detach-on-disconnect exists precisely so the\n  sandbox is *not* destroyed. Reclaiming it is the sweep's job.\n\nThere is no default cron, no background timer, and no warning at setup — a timer\ninside a library is wrong on every serverless platform the sandbox packages\ntarget. So treat scheduling as a hard requirement of wiring `durability`, on the\nsame footing as passing a real `LockStore`.\n\n## Why the reaper is correctness, not just cost\n\nThe cost story is the obvious one. The correctness story is the reason\n`'finalized'` exists as an outcome at all.\n\n`withPersistence` saves a run's transcript in **`onFinish`**. A run that\ncompletes while detached never reaches anyone's `onFinish`: the host that would\nhave run it is the host that left when the client disconnected. The agent\nfinished, its bytes are in the in-sandbox journal, the delivery log is frozen at\nthe last chunk that was actually delivered, and the message store has nothing.\n\nNothing recovers that on its own. A later takeover would — but a takeover only\nhappens if a user comes back, and the entire premise of a detached run is that\nthey might not. `reapDetachedRuns` is the actor that drives such a run through\n`chat()`'s normal middleware path so `onFinish` fires and the transcript lands.\nThat is what `'finalized'` means, and it is why a reaper you never schedule\nsilently loses completed work rather than merely wasting money.\n\n## `reapDetachedRuns`\n\n```ts\nimport { reapDetachedRuns } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\nOne sweep, in order:\n\n1. Ask `RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs: 0 })` — **once**. `ttlMs: 0` is\n   *every* detached run, because that is the candidate set for finalization: a\n   run that hit its sentinel one second after the viewer left has an unsaved\n   transcript and must not wait out the TTL. Expiry is then classified\n   in-process against `now - detachedRunTtlMs`, inclusively, so the store is not\n   asked twice to compute a subset.\n2. For each run in the batch (capped by `maxRuns`, default `25`): classify\n   expiry first, otherwise probe, and only then claim, quiesce, drive, and\n   reclaim.\n\n`listReclaimable` is optional on `RunStore`. A backend that omits it answers\n`{ considered: 0 }` and one log line — it cannot be reaped at all.\n\nThe function **never rejects**. It runs from a cron, an `alarm()`, or a\n`waitUntil`, where nobody is there to catch it, so every per-run failure is\nlogged and folded into the returned `ReapResult`.\n\n### Outcomes\n\n`ReapResult.runs` is one `ReapRunEntry` per run, and `ReapResult.outcomes` counts\nthem by `ReapRunOutcome`:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `finalized` | The probe saw the exit sentinel, the run was driven to terminal, the transcript is saved. The happy path. |\n| `expired` | Past `detachedRunTtlMs`. Cancelled first (so the teardown is an explicit cancel that destroys the sandbox), then driven to terminal. The probe is skipped — the outcome is terminal either way. Reported even when `runBudgetMs` is what ended the drive: on this path that is the mechanism, not an anomaly. The run's `status` tells the two apart — an agent that had already finished replays to `completed`, one still producing when the budget fired is `aborted`. |\n| `producing` | Still working. `pipeToRunLog` was never entered: nothing appended, no record written, `close()` not called, `detachedSince` untouched. |\n| `unknown` | The probe could not answer. Left exactly as untouched as `producing`, but an operator should see it. |\n| `budget-exceeded` | Anomaly, and **finalization only**. A run the journal already said was finished outran `runBudgetMs`. The record *is* terminal and the log *is* closed, so this is a diagnostic, not a leak. An expired run that outran its budget reports `expired` instead. The entry also carries `terminalizedAnyway`, which is set **if and only if** this anomaly happened — so it survives even when a subsequent failed reclaim overwrites the outcome. |\n| `not-claimed` | Another host holds the claim, or took it mid-drive. Normal — a real viewer attaching mid-sweep is exactly this. |\n| `reclaim-failed` | The run reached terminal and its transcript **is** saved — only `ReapOptions.reclaim` threw, so the sandbox is still up. **Not retryable by the sweep**: the record is terminal by now, so it has already left `listReclaimable` for good, and nothing will sweep it again. This is the outcome that says the cost leak the reaper exists to stop is still leaking; the entry's `error` is the only notice you get. The shipped `sandboxReclaimer` **rejects** on its `destroy-failed` arm precisely so this is reachable without a custom `reclaim`. Overwrites `budget-exceeded` when both happened — the leak is what needs acting on, and `terminalizedAnyway` preserves the other half. |\n| `failed` | Something threw. Logged, counted, and the sweep continued. |\n\nThere is deliberately no \"still running\" outcome distinct from `producing`, and\nno outcome meaning \"we drove it and it turned out not to be finished\" — see\nbelow for why that state is unreachable by construction.\n\n### It never drives a run to find out whether it finished\n\nThis is the one rule that shapes the whole module, and it is worth understanding\nbefore you wire anything, because the obvious alternative is worse than it looks.\n\nThe tempting design is: hand the run to `pipeToRunLog` under a short budget and\nsee whether it terminalizes. That does not work, because `pipeToRunLog` is total\nby construction — it **always** writes a terminal status and **always** calls\n`durability.close()`, on every path. Against a run that has *not* finished, all\nthree producer shapes are destructive:\n\n| The producer's reaction to the budget signal | Stored status | `close()` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ignores it and keeps producing | `aborted` | called |\n| Returns on abort (the realistic `drive`) | `aborted` | called |\n| Throws an `AbortError` | `failed` | called |\n\nThe middle row used to read `completed`, and that was the fatal one: a\nsignal-aware producer — the shape a *well-behaved* `drive` actually has — exits\nits loop normally, and `pipeToRunLog` checked its abort signal only per chunk, so\na healthy mid-flight run was recorded as `'completed'` with a `finishedAt`. That\ngap is fixed; `pipeToRunLog` re-checks the signal after the loop, so an aborted\ndrive is never recorded as completed.\n\nThe rule is unchanged, though, because the status was never the whole harm: every\nrow above writes a terminal record and closes a log that was deliberately left\nopen, ending every attached client's stream — and a terminal record drops out of\n`listReclaimable` **forever**, so TTL expiry can never reclaim that run's sandbox.\nA cost leak with no recovery path.\n\nSo finished-ness is learned **out of band**, from the in-sandbox journal, and\n`pipeToRunLog` is entered only for a run already known to have finished or one\nwhose TTL has expired (terminal either way). On the finalization path `runBudgetMs`\ntherefore degrades from a load-bearing mechanism into a safety net whose expiry is\na genuine anomaly. On the expiry path it stays load-bearing: nothing polls the\ncancel the reaper records, so the budget is what stops an expired run whose agent\nis still producing.\n\nThe sweep also **never clears `detachedSince`**. That field is what the reaper\nselects on and the evidence its TTL accounting used. Clearing it would reset the\nTTL on every sweep and a detached run would never expire. (The takeover path\nclears it, because there a real viewer genuinely stopped the clock.)\n\n## `probeRunExit`, and why `hasFinished` is injected\n\n`ReapOptions.hasFinished` is a required option you supply. `probeRunExit` is the\nshipped implementation:\n\n```ts\nimport { probeRunExit } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\nIt reads the **tail** of the run's journal (4 KB by default,\n`DEFAULT_EXIT_PROBE_BYTES`) and answers whether the `{\"__exit\":N}` sentinel is\nthere. Read-only: no append, no record write, no `close()`. Its three-armed\nanswer — `finished` / `producing` / `unknown` — is not a boolean on purpose, so a\nprovider `exec` that rejected can never be mistaken for \"the agent exited\". Any\nfailure answers `unknown`; an empty tail answers `producing`, the fail-safe\ndirection (a journal that does not exist yet is indistinguishable from one with\nno sentinel, and both mean *do not touch this run*).\n\nIt is injected rather than resolved inside the reaper for two reasons, both\nstructural:\n\n- **The delivery log cannot answer the question.** After a detach nothing appends\n  to it — the host that would have appended is the host that left — so the log is\n  frozen at the last delivered chunk while the *journal* keeps growing. The log\n  can only ever say \"no news\".\n- **Only your application can resolve a `SandboxHandle`.**\n  `SandboxInstanceStore` is `get` / `upsert` / `delete` with no `list` (see\n  [the named limitation](#the-limitation-there-is-no-instance-store-list)), and\n  mapping a `RunRecord.sandboxKey` to a live handle is application knowledge.\n\n`ReapOptions.reclaim` is injected for the same reason, and\n`sandboxReclaimer` is its ready-made implementation.\n\n## Server: wire the sweep once\n\nEverything the sweep needs is what your `POST` route already has, plus a way to\nresolve a sandbox handle from a key. Put it in one module and let each schedule\ncall it.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  probeRunExit,\n  reapDetachedRuns,\n  sandboxReclaimer,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { ReapResult, RunExitProbe } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your distributed LockStore, the same one `withSandbox` gets.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your persistence — the SAME RunStore the chat routes use.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result and the `SandboxInstanceStore` you passed to\n// `withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })`.\nimport { instances, sandbox } from './sandbox'\n// The same `drive` the attach route passes to `sandboxRunDriver` — a function of\n// `{ runId, threadId, signal }` that runs `chat()` with `durability.attach: true`.\n// See ./takeover, \"Server: take the run over\".\nimport { driveRun } from './drive-run'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n// The per-run log factory, and it MUST resolve the same log the producing route\n// wrote — otherwise the sweep terminalizes an empty log while the real one stays\n// open. A cron has no incoming request, so synthesize one naming the run.\n//\n// `?runId` is the form to use here: `durableStream` resolves a run from the\n// `X-Run-Id` header first, then `?runId` (the same precedence core's own\n// `memoryStream` uses), but a synthesized `Request` has no reason to carry a\n// header when a query param is just as easy to set — either addresses the\n// same `agent-runs/<runId>` the chat routes' `durableStream(request,\n// durableOptions)` addresses. No resume offset is set, because the reaper is\n// a producer, not a replaying client.\n//\n// The same backend and options the chat routes use — see ../resumable-streams/\n// advanced for the full option set. `durableStream` talks to it over HTTP, so a\n// synthesized request works exactly like a real one: the run's state lives in\n// the backend, not in this process.\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}\n\nfunction durabilityFor(runId: string) {\n  const url = new URL('https://reaper.internal/')\n  url.searchParams.set('runId', runId)\n  return durableStream(new Request(url), durableOptions)\n}\n\n// Only the application can map a recorded `sandboxKey` back to a live handle:\n// the instance store answers `get(key)` but never enumerates, and resolving the\n// provider sandbox id it holds is your provider's `resume`. Anything this cannot\n// answer must be `unknown`, never `finished`.\nasync function hasFinished(record: RunRecord): Promise<RunExitProbe> {\n  if (record.sandboxKey === undefined) return { state: 'unknown' }\n  try {\n    const instance = await instances.get(record.sandboxKey)\n    if (instance === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    const handle = await sandbox.provider.resume({\n      id: instance.providerSandboxId,\n    })\n    if (handle === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    return await probeRunExit({ handle, runId: record.runId })\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { state: 'unknown', error }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function sweepDetachedRuns(): Promise<ReapResult> {\n  return reapDetachedRuns({\n    runs,\n    locks,\n    durability: durabilityFor,\n    hasFinished,\n    drive: driveRun,\n    now: Date.now(),\n    // The only place this TTL is configured — there is no `withSandbox`\n    // equivalent. In milliseconds.\n    detachedRunTtlMs: 30 * 60 * 1000,\n    // Sequential by design — each run costs a lock, a provider round-trip, and a\n    // full replay. Keep the batch inside your platform's invocation budget.\n    maxRuns: 25,\n    reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({\n      provider: sandbox.provider,\n      instances,\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`reapDetachedRuns` resolves rather than rejects, so log the summary and let the\nschedule keep its cadence:\n\n```ts\nimport type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// Plain Node, no platform cron. One in flight at a time: a sweep that overruns\n// its interval must not be started twice, or two invocations race for the same\n// claims and every second one reports `not-claimed`.\nlet inFlight = false\n\nasync function tick(): Promise<void> {\n  if (inFlight) return\n  inFlight = true\n  try {\n    const result: ReapResult = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n    console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    for (const run of result.runs) {\n      if (run.outcome === 'failed' || run.outcome === 'unknown') {\n        console.warn('reap needs attention', run.runId, run.outcome, run.error)\n      }\n    }\n  } finally {\n    inFlight = false\n  }\n}\n\nsetInterval(() => void tick(), 60_000)\n```\n\n### Vercel Cron\n\nA cron route is a plain `GET`. Guard it — it drives runs and destroys sandboxes,\nso it must not be publicly callable.\n\n```ts\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const secret = process.env.CRON_SECRET\n  if (\n    secret === undefined ||\n    request.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${secret}`\n  ) {\n    return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n  return Response.json({\n    considered: result.considered,\n    probed: result.probed,\n    outcomes: result.outcomes,\n  })\n}\n```\n\nRegister it in `vercel.json` with a `path` and a `schedule` (`*/5 * * * *` is a\nreasonable start — see [sizing](#sizing-detachedrunttl-and-the-sweep-interval)).\n\n### Cloudflare: a Durable Object `alarm()`\n\nA DO alarm is the natural scheduler on Workers: it is single-instance, so the\n\"one sweep in flight\" guard is free, and it re-arms itself.\n\n```ts\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// The slice of `DurableObjectState` this needs. In a real Worker this is\n// `state.storage` from your Workers types.\ninterface AlarmStorage {\n  setAlarm: (scheduledTime: number) => Promise<void>\n}\n\nexport class RunReaper {\n  constructor(private readonly storage: AlarmStorage) {}\n\n  async alarm(): Promise<void> {\n    try {\n      const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n      console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    } finally {\n      // Re-arm in `finally`. An alarm that throws without rescheduling stops\n      // reaping forever, which is exactly the failure mode this page is about.\n      await this.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 60_000)\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Cloudflare's stall watchdog is not a reaper\n\n`createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ stallTimeoutMs })` sets an app-wide policy for\nboth `do-drives` and `colocated` modes. Omit it for the `300000` ms default, pass\na positive safe-integer millisecond value to customize it, or pass `false` to\ndisable it. Its alarm checks about every 30 seconds.\n\nPersisted events and authenticated `/_bridge` or `/tool-exec` callback arrival\nand completion refresh the run record's `updatedAt`; unknown-run and\nunauthorized requests do not. Silent native operations therefore need a timeout\nlonger than their longest expected quiet period. In-flight authenticated\ncallbacks are protected. A callback that never completes can leave an orphaned\n`running` record. Disabling the watchdog with `false` can do the same.\n\nThe watchdog atomically marks a stale log `failed`, but may not kill the\nunderlying process and never probes a journal or reclaims a sandbox. You must\nstill schedule `sweepDetachedRuns`; see the\n[Cloudflare stall-watchdog details](./cloudflare#stall-watchdog).\n\n## `pruneJournals`: bounding the journal directory\n\nThe reaper terminalizes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does not tidy the\njournal directory *inside* a sandbox that is still alive — a `keepAlive` sandbox\nserving many turns accumulates journals for every run whose exit sentinel nobody\nwas there to observe.\n\n```ts\nimport { pruneJournals } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { handleForSandbox } from './sandbox-handles'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function sweepJournals(sandboxKey: string) {\n  const result = await pruneJournals({\n    handle: await handleForSandbox(sandboxKey),\n    // Only `get` is used: the sweep asks about the runIds it found on disk and\n    // never enumerates the store, so no optional `RunStore` method is needed.\n    runs,\n  })\n  if (result.ageGate === 'unavailable') {\n    console.warn('journal sweep could not age-gate; kept every orphan')\n  }\n  return result\n}\n```\n\n**It fails closed everywhere.** The journal is the only copy of the bytes a\nsuccessor host needs to replay a run a dead host abandoned, so the decision\nprocedure is not \"delete unless there is a reason to keep\" — it is the opposite,\nand every arm that is not a *proven*-safe deletion keeps:\n\n| What the store says | Action | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal (`isTerminalRunStatus`) | **delete** | The delivery log, not the journal, is the record a late takeover aligns against. A non-zero exit is terminal too. |\n| Non-terminal — including `'interrupted'` | keep | An interrupt is a human-in-the-loop *pause*; interrupt-resume continues from that journal. |\n| Nothing (unknown runId) | keep until `orphanTtlMs` | The reader **creates** the journal before the record exists, so \"unknown runId\" is the normal state of a run that started moments ago. |\n| The lookup threw | keep | An unanswered question is not a licence to delete. |\n| The filename did not decode | keep | A truncated name decodes to a plausible but *wrong* runId, so asking the store would answer about some other — possibly live — run. |\n| The mtime age gate is unavailable | keep every age-gated entry | Cannot age-gate ⇒ cannot expire. `unavailable` is a first-class result, never an empty listing. |\n\nThat last row is the trap the module exists to not fall into. BusyBox `find`\nprints its \"unrecognized option\" diagnostic to stderr and exits **1 with empty\nstdout**; code that reads that as \"no file is newer than the cutoff\" and\nconcludes \"therefore every file is old\" deletes the whole directory, live runs\nincluded. The mtime listing carries a self-witness line and reports\n`unavailable` rather than `[]`, and `pruneJournals` honors that as \"I keep\".\n\nDeletions per sweep are capped by `maxDeletes` (default `DEFAULT_MAX_DELETES`,\n200); the remainder is reported as kept with reason `max-deletes` and picked up\nnext time. `orphanTtlMs` defaults to `DEFAULT_ORPHAN_TTL_MS` (one hour) —\nthree orders of magnitude of headroom on the create-then-record race, because the\ncost of too long is bytes and the cost of too short is a destroyed live run.\n`pruneJournals` never rejects either; failures land in\n`PruneJournalsResult.failures`.\n\n## `reclaimSandbox` and `sandboxReclaimer`\n\n```ts\nimport { reclaimSandbox, sandboxReclaimer } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\n`reclaimSandbox(record, { provider, instances })` destroys the sandbox a terminal\nrun was bound to, using `RunRecord.sandboxKey` — recorded by the detach path at\nthe moment it still knew the compound key, because the reaper has none of the\ninputs (`threadId`, workspace hash, tenant, reuse strategy) needed to re-derive\nit. It answers `'destroyed'`, `'destroy-failed'`, `'no-sandbox-key'`,\n`'not-found'`, or `'provider-mismatch'`.\n\nTwo orderings are load-bearing:\n\n- **The provider check comes first**, before either `destroy` or `delete`. A\n  multi-provider app would otherwise hand a Docker container id to Daytona's\n  `destroy`, which at best errors and at worst matches an unrelated sandbox in\n  the other provider's id namespace. A mismatch therefore touches **nothing**,\n  including the instance record, which the *right* provider still needs.\n- **`destroy` before `delete`, and `delete` regardless of whether `destroy`\n  threw.** The provider sandbox may already be gone (idle-reclaimed, region\n  wiped, container pruned). Keeping an instance record that points at nothing\n  guarantees a failed `resume` on the thread's next turn — a broken user\n  experience — whereas an orphaned provider sandbox is a bounded cost the\n  provider itself reclaims. `delete` is therefore unconditional, but the\n  *outcome* must not claim success when `destroy` threw: `'destroy-failed'` is\n  reported instead of `'destroyed'`, because the instance record is gone\n  either way and an operator needs to tell \"torn down cleanly\" apart from\n  \"possibly still billing, and now unreachable from here since\n  `SandboxInstanceStore` has no `list`\". `sandboxReclaimer` logs\n  `'destroy-failed'` above debug level for exactly that reason — every other\n  outcome is bookkeeping an operator never needs to see.\n\n`sandboxReclaimer(options)` is the same thing adapted to `ReapOptions.reclaim`:\nit logs the outcome and resolves — **except on `'destroy-failed'`, where it\nrejects** with a `SandboxReclaimFailedError`. The reaper calls it **only** once\nthe record actually reached a terminal status, and with the *originally listed*\nrecord rather than the one the drive returned — a failed terminal `update`\nyields a locally rebuilt record with no `sandboxKey`, which would answer\n`'no-sandbox-key'` and leak the sandbox silently on exactly the path where\nsomething already went wrong.\n\nThat rejection is deliberate, and it is what makes the `reclaim-failed` outcome\nreachable at all. `ReapOptions.reclaim` is `(record) => Promise<void>`, so a\nrejection is the sweep's only channel for \"the sandbox was NOT reclaimed\":\n\nWire the reclaimer as shown above (`reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({ provider,\ninstances })`), then inspect the summary the sweep hands back:\n\n```ts\nimport { SandboxReclaimFailedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nexport function alertOnLeakedSandboxes(\n  result: ReapResult,\n  alert: (message: string, detail: Record<string, unknown>) => void,\n): void {\n  // Watch this counter — it is the leak alarm.\n  if (result.outcomes['reclaim-failed'] === 0) return\n\n  for (const run of result.runs) {\n    if (run.outcome !== 'reclaim-failed') continue\n    // The transcript IS saved and the record IS terminal; only the teardown\n    // failed. `status`/`exitCode` are reported exactly as `finalized` reports\n    // them, which is what distinguishes this from a `failed` sweep.\n    const leakedKey =\n      run.error instanceof SandboxReclaimFailedError\n        ? run.error.sandboxKey\n        : undefined\n\n    alert('sandbox may still be billing', {\n      runId: run.runId,\n      status: run.status,\n      sandboxKey: leakedKey,\n      // Present ONLY if the drive also outran `runBudgetMs`. `reclaim-failed`\n      // overwrites the `budget-exceeded` outcome, so this field is what keeps\n      // that second diagnostic on the entry.\n      budgetAnomaly: run.terminalizedAnyway !== undefined,\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA `reclaim-failed` run has already left `listReclaimable` for good, so no later\nsweep retries it — that entry is the only notice you will get. A custom\n`reclaim` should follow the same convention: reject when the sandbox was not\ntorn down, resolve when there was nothing to tear down.\n\n## Sizing `detachedRunTtlMs` and the sweep interval\n\n`detachedRunTtlMs` is a wall-clock cap, in milliseconds, on a *running agent\nwith nobody watching*. There is no default and no string parsing: it is a\nrequired, plain number on `ReapOptions`, and deliberately lives only there —\n`withSandbox` cannot enforce a TTL itself, since `reapDetachedRuns` runs from a\ncron with no chat request in flight and has no capability bus to read it\nfrom. Passing it directly to the sweep is what keeps it a single source of\ntruth instead of two settings that could silently disagree.\n\nSize it from your agent's honest p99 task duration, not from user patience: too\nshort and you cancel real work a user was going to come back to; too long and an\nabandoned run bills for that long. If a coding agent legitimately runs 20\nminutes, 30 minutes (`30 * 60 * 1000`) is tight and an hour is defensible.\n\nThen keep the sweep interval well **under** the TTL. The interval bounds how\nlong *past* the TTL an expired run survives, and it also bounds how long a run\nthat finished while detached waits before its transcript lands. A one-minute\nalarm against a 30-minute TTL costs one cheap store query per minute and makes\nboth windows negligible. Sweeping every 30 minutes against a 30-minute TTL means\nan expiry can be an hour late.\n\n`maxRuns` (default 25) and the sequential per-run loop exist so one invocation\ncannot outrun a Worker's CPU budget or a Lambda timeout and be killed mid-drive.\nIf a backlog exceeds the batch, the next tick takes the next batch — reduce the\ninterval rather than raising the cap.\n\n## Retention: three clocks, and only one of them is ours\n\nThe reaper closes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does **not** garbage-collect\nyour stored data, and the split is deliberate:\n\n- **Event-log retention is your durability backend's job**, inside whatever\n  `StreamDurability` you wired. The framework never deletes from it, because it\n  does not own the storage — `memoryStream` evicts completed runs after a grace\n  window, `durableStream` retains per its backend's own policy, and a custom\n  adapter retains per yours. **30 days is a reasonable default**: long enough\n  that a user who reopens a thread the next morning still gets an exact replay of\n  the run, short enough that a chatty deployment does not accumulate raw chunk\n  logs forever.\n- **Message retention is the message store's job**, and it must **outlive** event\n  retention. The transcript is the durable artifact; the event log is a delivery\n  detail. Once a run's events age out, the thread must still render — see the\n  client half below.\n- **Journals are bounded inside the sandbox** by `pruneJournals`, and vanish with\n  the sandbox when `reclaimSandbox` destroys it.\n\nThe ordering is what matters: events may age out first, messages must not.\n\n## Client: tolerate an aged-out event log\n\nThe usual case needs nothing. `useChat` with `persistence: true` fetches the\ntranscript on mount and only then tails whatever run is still generating, so a\nrun whose event log is gone simply paints from messages.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Thread({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  // On mount this GETs the transcript from the message store, then tails an\n  // `activeRun` if `reconstructChat` reports one. An aged-out event log means\n  // the tail yields nothing — the transcript is already painted, so the thread\n  // still renders correctly. This is why message retention must outlive event\n  // retention.\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{messages.length} messages</p>\n      <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Continue')}>Continue</button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you drive the rejoin yourself, make the fallback explicit: join the run, and\nif it yields nothing, fall back to the stored transcript rather than showing an\nempty thread.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ThreadView({ threadId, runId }: {\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState('joining')\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function join(): Promise<void> {\n      let received = 0\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        received += 1\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n      // Zero chunks from a run the server knew about means its delivery log\n      // aged out (or was pruned). The transcript is still authoritative.\n      if (received === 0) setStatus('replaced-by-transcript')\n      else setStatus('joined')\n    }\n\n    void join().catch(() => setStatus('replaced-by-transcript'))\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [runId])\n\n  // Reconstructed from the message store — always render this, and let the\n  // joined events refine it. Never gate the thread on the event log existing.\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>\n        {status}: {chunks.length} live events, thread {threadId}\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe same rule applies server-side: a `GET` that only replays the log will serve\nan empty stream for an aged-out run. Route reconstruct-from-messages first and\nresume second, as [Persistence Overview](../persistence/overview) shows.\n\n## The limitation: there is no instance-store `list`\n\n`SandboxInstanceStore` is `get` / `upsert` / `delete`, with no enumeration, and\nthat is a deliberate refusal: adding `list` would force every backend and the\nconformance suite to grow an enumeration for one hypothetical caller.\n\nThe consequence is real and documented rather than hidden. **A sandbox whose run\nrecord was deleted before any sweep saw it is unreachable.** Nothing can name its\nkey, so `reclaimSandbox` can never be called for it, and it survives until the\nprovider's own idle reclamation takes it. Two things keep that rare: prune run\nrecords only after their runs are terminal *and* their sandboxes reclaimed, and\nset a provider-side idle timeout as the backstop.\n\n## See also\n\n- [Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs): where this sweep fits in the whole\n  picture, in plain language and with no code\n- [Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover): how a run becomes detached in the first\n  place, `sandboxRunDriver`, and the single-writer fencing the sweep reuses\n- [The Run Journal](./journal): the journal `probeRunExit` reads and\n  `pruneJournals` bounds\n- [Store Reference](../persistence/store-reference):\n  `listReclaimable`, and the run fields a reapable backend must round-trip\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](./durability): the instance store\n  `reclaimSandbox` deletes from\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Reaping & Retention (Advanced)\nid: sandbox-reaping\norder: 14\ndescription: \"Schedule the sweep that finalizes detached runs, prunes journals, and reclaims sandboxes — and understand why a durable run that nothing reaps never lands its transcript.\"\nkeywords:\n  - reapDetachedRuns\n  - probeRunExit\n  - pruneJournals\n  - reclaimSandbox\n  - sandboxReclaimer\n  - SandboxReclaimFailedError\n  - detachedRunTtlMs\n  - retention\n---\n\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover) makes a disconnect survivable: the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. That is only half a lifecycle. Something has to be the *end* of a run\nthat no viewer ever came back for.\n\nThat something is `reapDetachedRuns`, and it is a **function, not a scheduler**.\n\n## Read this first: nothing schedules it for you\n\nAn application that wires `runs` + `durability` and never schedules\n`reapDetachedRuns` is not running a reduced version of this feature. It is\nrunning a broken one, in three distinct ways at once:\n\n- **No detached run's delivery log is ever closed.** A detached run's log is\n  deliberately left OPEN and un-terminalized so a takeover can continue it. If\n  nothing ever terminalizes it, every client that attaches parks forever waiting\n  for an event that will not arrive.\n- **`detachedRunTtlMs` is enforced by nothing.** It is not a timer. It is a\n  cutoff the sweep compares `detachedSince` against, and it exists only as an\n  argument to `reapDetachedRuns` — there is no `withSandbox` equivalent. With no\n  sweep, it is a number nobody ever reads and an abandoned agent burns tokens\n  until something else kills it.\n- **Sandboxes bill indefinitely.** Detach-on-disconnect exists precisely so the\n  sandbox is *not* destroyed. Reclaiming it is the sweep's job.\n\nThere is no default cron, no background timer, and no warning at setup — a timer\ninside a library is wrong on every serverless platform the sandbox packages\ntarget. So treat scheduling as a hard requirement of wiring `durability`, on the\nsame footing as passing a real `LockStore`.\n\n## Why the reaper is correctness, not just cost\n\nThe cost story is the obvious one. The correctness story is the reason\n`'finalized'` exists as an outcome at all.\n\n`withPersistence` saves a run's transcript in **`onFinish`**. A run that\ncompletes while detached never reaches anyone's `onFinish`: the host that would\nhave run it is the host that left when the client disconnected. The agent\nfinished, its bytes are in the in-sandbox journal, the delivery log is frozen at\nthe last chunk that was actually delivered, and the message store has nothing.\n\nNothing recovers that on its own. A later takeover would — but a takeover only\nhappens if a user comes back, and the entire premise of a detached run is that\nthey might not. `reapDetachedRuns` is the actor that drives such a run through\n`chat()`'s normal middleware path so `onFinish` fires and the transcript lands.\nThat is what `'finalized'` means, and it is why a reaper you never schedule\nsilently loses completed work rather than merely wasting money.\n\n## `reapDetachedRuns`\n\n```ts\nimport { reapDetachedRuns } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\nOne sweep, in order:\n\n1. Ask `RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs: 0 })` — **once**. `ttlMs: 0` is\n   *every* detached run, because that is the candidate set for finalization: a\n   run that hit its sentinel one second after the viewer left has an unsaved\n   transcript and must not wait out the TTL. Expiry is then classified\n   in-process against `now - detachedRunTtlMs`, inclusively, so the store is not\n   asked twice to compute a subset.\n2. For each run in the batch (capped by `maxRuns`, default `25`): classify\n   expiry first, otherwise probe, and only then claim, quiesce, drive, and\n   reclaim.\n\n`listReclaimable` is optional on `RunStore`. A backend that omits it answers\n`{ considered: 0 }` and one log line — it cannot be reaped at all.\n\nThe function **never rejects**. It runs from a cron, an `alarm()`, or a\n`waitUntil`, where nobody is there to catch it, so every per-run failure is\nlogged and folded into the returned `ReapResult`.\n\n### Outcomes\n\n`ReapResult.runs` is one `ReapRunEntry` per run, and `ReapResult.outcomes` counts\nthem by `ReapRunOutcome`:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `finalized` | The probe saw the exit sentinel, the run was driven to terminal, the transcript is saved. The happy path. |\n| `expired` | Past `detachedRunTtlMs`. Cancelled first (so the teardown is an explicit cancel that destroys the sandbox), then driven to terminal. The probe is skipped — the outcome is terminal either way. Reported even when `runBudgetMs` is what ended the drive: on this path that is the mechanism, not an anomaly. The run's `status` tells the two apart — an agent that had already finished replays to `completed`, one still producing when the budget fired is `aborted`. |\n| `producing` | Still working. `pipeToRunLog` was never entered: nothing appended, no record written, `close()` not called, `detachedSince` untouched. |\n| `unknown` | The probe could not answer. Left exactly as untouched as `producing`, but an operator should see it. |\n| `budget-exceeded` | Anomaly, and **finalization only**. A run the journal already said was finished outran `runBudgetMs`. The record *is* terminal and the log *is* closed, so this is a diagnostic, not a leak. An expired run that outran its budget reports `expired` instead. The entry also carries `terminalizedAnyway`, which is set **if and only if** this anomaly happened — so it survives even when a subsequent failed reclaim overwrites the outcome. |\n| `not-claimed` | Another host holds the claim, or took it mid-drive. Normal — a real viewer attaching mid-sweep is exactly this. |\n| `reclaim-failed` | The run reached terminal and its transcript **is** saved — only `ReapOptions.reclaim` threw, so the sandbox is still up. **Not retryable by the sweep**: the record is terminal by now, so it has already left `listReclaimable` for good, and nothing will sweep it again. This is the outcome that says the cost leak the reaper exists to stop is still leaking; the entry's `error` is the only notice you get. The shipped `sandboxReclaimer` **rejects** on its `destroy-failed` arm precisely so this is reachable without a custom `reclaim`. Overwrites `budget-exceeded` when both happened — the leak is what needs acting on, and `terminalizedAnyway` preserves the other half. |\n| `failed` | Something threw. Logged, counted, and the sweep continued. |\n\nThere is deliberately no \"still running\" outcome distinct from `producing`, and\nno outcome meaning \"we drove it and it turned out not to be finished\" — see\nbelow for why that state is unreachable by construction.\n\n### It never drives a run to find out whether it finished\n\nThis is the one rule that shapes the whole module, and it is worth understanding\nbefore you wire anything, because the obvious alternative is worse than it looks.\n\nThe tempting design is: hand the run to `pipeToRunLog` under a short budget and\nsee whether it terminalizes. That does not work, because `pipeToRunLog` is total\nby construction — it **always** writes a terminal status and **always** calls\n`durability.close()`, on every path. Against a run that has *not* finished, all\nthree producer shapes are destructive:\n\n| The producer's reaction to the budget signal | Stored status | `close()` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ignores it and keeps producing | `aborted` | called |\n| Returns on abort (the realistic `drive`) | `aborted` | called |\n| Throws an `AbortError` | `failed` | called |\n\nThe middle row used to read `completed`, and that was the fatal one: a\nsignal-aware producer — the shape a *well-behaved* `drive` actually has — exits\nits loop normally, and `pipeToRunLog` checked its abort signal only per chunk, so\na healthy mid-flight run was recorded as `'completed'` with a `finishedAt`. That\ngap is fixed; `pipeToRunLog` re-checks the signal after the loop, so an aborted\ndrive is never recorded as completed.\n\nThe rule is unchanged, though, because the status was never the whole harm: every\nrow above writes a terminal record and closes a log that was deliberately left\nopen, ending every attached client's stream — and a terminal record drops out of\n`listReclaimable` **forever**, so TTL expiry can never reclaim that run's sandbox.\nA cost leak with no recovery path.\n\nSo finished-ness is learned **out of band**, from the in-sandbox journal, and\n`pipeToRunLog` is entered only for a run already known to have finished or one\nwhose TTL has expired (terminal either way). On the finalization path `runBudgetMs`\ntherefore degrades from a load-bearing mechanism into a safety net whose expiry is\na genuine anomaly. On the expiry path it stays load-bearing: nothing polls the\ncancel the reaper records, so the budget is what stops an expired run whose agent\nis still producing.\n\nThe sweep also **never clears `detachedSince`**. That field is what the reaper\nselects on and the evidence its TTL accounting used. Clearing it would reset the\nTTL on every sweep and a detached run would never expire. (The takeover path\nclears it, because there a real viewer genuinely stopped the clock.)\n\n## `probeRunExit`, and why `hasFinished` is injected\n\n`ReapOptions.hasFinished` is a required option you supply. `probeRunExit` is the\nshipped implementation:\n\n```ts\nimport { probeRunExit } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\nIt reads the **tail** of the run's journal (4 KB by default,\n`DEFAULT_EXIT_PROBE_BYTES`) and answers whether the `{\"__exit\":N}` sentinel is\nthere. Read-only: no append, no record write, no `close()`. Its three-armed\nanswer — `finished` / `producing` / `unknown` — is not a boolean on purpose, so a\nprovider `exec` that rejected can never be mistaken for \"the agent exited\". Any\nfailure answers `unknown`; an empty tail answers `producing`, the fail-safe\ndirection (a journal that does not exist yet is indistinguishable from one with\nno sentinel, and both mean *do not touch this run*).\n\nIt is injected rather than resolved inside the reaper for two reasons, both\nstructural:\n\n- **The delivery log cannot answer the question.** After a detach nothing appends\n  to it — the host that would have appended is the host that left — so the log is\n  frozen at the last delivered chunk while the *journal* keeps growing. The log\n  can only ever say \"no news\".\n- **Only your application can resolve a `SandboxHandle`.**\n  `SandboxInstanceStore` is `get` / `upsert` / `delete` with no `list` (see\n  [the named limitation](#the-limitation-there-is-no-instance-store-list)), and\n  mapping a `RunRecord.sandboxKey` to a live handle is application knowledge.\n\n`ReapOptions.reclaim` is injected for the same reason, and\n`sandboxReclaimer` is its ready-made implementation.\n\n## Server: wire the sweep once\n\nEverything the sweep needs is what your `POST` route already has, plus a way to\nresolve a sandbox handle from a key. Put it in one module and let each schedule\ncall it.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  probeRunExit,\n  reapDetachedRuns,\n  sandboxReclaimer,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { ReapResult, RunExitProbe } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your distributed LockStore, the same one `withSandbox` gets.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your persistence — the SAME RunStore the chat routes use.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result and the `SandboxInstanceStore` you passed to\n// `withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })`.\nimport { instances, sandbox } from './sandbox'\n// The same `drive` the attach route passes to `sandboxRunDriver` — a function of\n// `{ runId, threadId, signal }` that runs `chat()` with `durability.attach: true`.\n// See ./takeover, \"Server: take the run over\".\nimport { driveRun } from './drive-run'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n// The per-run log factory, and it MUST resolve the same log the producing route\n// wrote — otherwise the sweep terminalizes an empty log while the real one stays\n// open. A cron has no incoming request, so synthesize one naming the run.\n//\n// `?runId` is the form to use here: `durableStream` resolves a run from the\n// `X-Run-Id` header first, then `?runId` (the same precedence core's own\n// `memoryStream` uses), but a synthesized `Request` has no reason to carry a\n// header when a query param is just as easy to set — either addresses the\n// same `agent-runs/<runId>` the chat routes' `durableStream(request,\n// durableOptions)` addresses. No resume offset is set, because the reaper is\n// a producer, not a replaying client.\n//\n// The same backend and options the chat routes use — see ../resumable-streams/\n// advanced for the full option set. `durableStream` talks to it over HTTP, so a\n// synthesized request works exactly like a real one: the run's state lives in\n// the backend, not in this process.\nconst durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}\n\nfunction durabilityFor(runId: string) {\n  const url = new URL('https://reaper.internal/')\n  url.searchParams.set('runId', runId)\n  return durableStream(new Request(url), durableOptions)\n}\n\n// Only the application can map a recorded `sandboxKey` back to a live handle:\n// the instance store answers `get(key)` but never enumerates, and resolving the\n// provider sandbox id it holds is your provider's `resume`. Anything this cannot\n// answer must be `unknown`, never `finished`.\nasync function hasFinished(record: RunRecord): Promise<RunExitProbe> {\n  if (record.sandboxKey === undefined) return { state: 'unknown' }\n  try {\n    const instance = await instances.get(record.sandboxKey)\n    if (instance === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    const handle = await sandbox.provider.resume({\n      id: instance.providerSandboxId,\n    })\n    if (handle === null) return { state: 'unknown' }\n    return await probeRunExit({ handle, runId: record.runId })\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { state: 'unknown', error }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function sweepDetachedRuns(): Promise<ReapResult> {\n  return reapDetachedRuns({\n    runs,\n    locks,\n    durability: durabilityFor,\n    hasFinished,\n    drive: driveRun,\n    now: Date.now(),\n    // The only place this TTL is configured — there is no `withSandbox`\n    // equivalent. In milliseconds.\n    detachedRunTtlMs: 30 * 60 * 1000,\n    // Sequential by design — each run costs a lock, a provider round-trip, and a\n    // full replay. Keep the batch inside your platform's invocation budget.\n    maxRuns: 25,\n    reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({\n      provider: sandbox.provider,\n      instances,\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\n`reapDetachedRuns` resolves rather than rejects, so log the summary and let the\nschedule keep its cadence:\n\n```ts\nimport type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// Plain Node, no platform cron. One in flight at a time: a sweep that overruns\n// its interval must not be started twice, or two invocations race for the same\n// claims and every second one reports `not-claimed`.\nlet inFlight = false\n\nasync function tick(): Promise<void> {\n  if (inFlight) return\n  inFlight = true\n  try {\n    const result: ReapResult = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n    console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    for (const run of result.runs) {\n      if (run.outcome === 'failed' || run.outcome === 'unknown') {\n        console.warn('reap needs attention', run.runId, run.outcome, run.error)\n      }\n    }\n  } finally {\n    inFlight = false\n  }\n}\n\nsetInterval(() => void tick(), 60_000)\n```\n\n### Vercel Cron\n\nA cron route is a plain `GET`. Guard it — it drives runs and destroys sandboxes,\nso it must not be publicly callable.\n\n```ts\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const secret = process.env.CRON_SECRET\n  if (\n    secret === undefined ||\n    request.headers.get('authorization') !== `Bearer ${secret}`\n  ) {\n    return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })\n  }\n  const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n  return Response.json({\n    considered: result.considered,\n    probed: result.probed,\n    outcomes: result.outcomes,\n  })\n}\n```\n\nRegister it in `vercel.json` with a `path` and a `schedule` (`*/5 * * * *` is a\nreasonable start — see [sizing](#sizing-detachedrunttl-and-the-sweep-interval)).\n\n### Cloudflare: a Durable Object `alarm()`\n\nA DO alarm is the natural scheduler on Workers: it is single-instance, so the\n\"one sweep in flight\" guard is free, and it re-arms itself.\n\n```ts\nimport { sweepDetachedRuns } from './sweep'\n\n// The slice of `DurableObjectState` this needs. In a real Worker this is\n// `state.storage` from your Workers types.\ninterface AlarmStorage {\n  setAlarm: (scheduledTime: number) => Promise<void>\n}\n\nexport class RunReaper {\n  constructor(private readonly storage: AlarmStorage) {}\n\n  async alarm(): Promise<void> {\n    try {\n      const result = await sweepDetachedRuns()\n      console.log('reap', result.considered, result.outcomes)\n    } finally {\n      // Re-arm in `finally`. An alarm that throws without rescheduling stops\n      // reaping forever, which is exactly the failure mode this page is about.\n      await this.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 60_000)\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Cloudflare's stall watchdog is not a reaper\n\n`createCloudflareSandboxAgent({ stallTimeoutMs })` sets an app-wide policy for\nboth `do-drives` and `colocated` modes. Omit it for the `300000` ms default, pass\na positive safe-integer millisecond value to customize it, or pass `false` to\ndisable it. Its alarm checks about every 30 seconds.\n\nPersisted events and authenticated `/_bridge` or `/tool-exec` callback arrival\nand completion refresh the run record's `updatedAt`; unknown-run and\nunauthorized requests do not. Silent native operations therefore need a timeout\nlonger than their longest expected quiet period. In-flight authenticated\ncallbacks are protected. A callback that never completes can leave an orphaned\n`running` record. Disabling the watchdog with `false` can do the same.\n\nThe watchdog atomically marks a stale log `failed`, but may not kill the\nunderlying process and never probes a journal or reclaims a sandbox. You must\nstill schedule `sweepDetachedRuns`; see the\n[Cloudflare stall-watchdog details](./cloudflare#stall-watchdog).\n\n## `pruneJournals`: bounding the journal directory\n\nThe reaper terminalizes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does not tidy the\njournal directory *inside* a sandbox that is still alive — a `keepAlive` sandbox\nserving many turns accumulates journals for every run whose exit sentinel nobody\nwas there to observe.\n\n```ts\nimport { pruneJournals } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { handleForSandbox } from './sandbox-handles'\n\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function sweepJournals(sandboxKey: string) {\n  const result = await pruneJournals({\n    handle: await handleForSandbox(sandboxKey),\n    // Only `get` is used: the sweep asks about the runIds it found on disk and\n    // never enumerates the store, so no optional `RunStore` method is needed.\n    runs,\n  })\n  if (result.ageGate === 'unavailable') {\n    console.warn('journal sweep could not age-gate; kept every orphan')\n  }\n  return result\n}\n```\n\n**It fails closed everywhere.** The journal is the only copy of the bytes a\nsuccessor host needs to replay a run a dead host abandoned, so the decision\nprocedure is not \"delete unless there is a reason to keep\" — it is the opposite,\nand every arm that is not a *proven*-safe deletion keeps:\n\n| What the store says | Action | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal (`isTerminalRunStatus`) | **delete** | The delivery log, not the journal, is the record a late takeover aligns against. A non-zero exit is terminal too. |\n| Non-terminal — including `'interrupted'` | keep | An interrupt is a human-in-the-loop *pause*; interrupt-resume continues from that journal. |\n| Nothing (unknown runId) | keep until `orphanTtlMs` | The reader **creates** the journal before the record exists, so \"unknown runId\" is the normal state of a run that started moments ago. |\n| The lookup threw | keep | An unanswered question is not a licence to delete. |\n| The filename did not decode | keep | A truncated name decodes to a plausible but *wrong* runId, so asking the store would answer about some other — possibly live — run. |\n| The mtime age gate is unavailable | keep every age-gated entry | Cannot age-gate ⇒ cannot expire. `unavailable` is a first-class result, never an empty listing. |\n\nThat last row is the trap the module exists to not fall into. BusyBox `find`\nprints its \"unrecognized option\" diagnostic to stderr and exits **1 with empty\nstdout**; code that reads that as \"no file is newer than the cutoff\" and\nconcludes \"therefore every file is old\" deletes the whole directory, live runs\nincluded. The mtime listing carries a self-witness line and reports\n`unavailable` rather than `[]`, and `pruneJournals` honors that as \"I keep\".\n\nDeletions per sweep are capped by `maxDeletes` (default `DEFAULT_MAX_DELETES`,\n200); the remainder is reported as kept with reason `max-deletes` and picked up\nnext time. `orphanTtlMs` defaults to `DEFAULT_ORPHAN_TTL_MS` (one hour) —\nthree orders of magnitude of headroom on the create-then-record race, because the\ncost of too long is bytes and the cost of too short is a destroyed live run.\n`pruneJournals` never rejects either; failures land in\n`PruneJournalsResult.failures`.\n\n## `reclaimSandbox` and `sandboxReclaimer`\n\n```ts\nimport { reclaimSandbox, sandboxReclaimer } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n```\n\n`reclaimSandbox(record, { provider, instances })` destroys the sandbox a terminal\nrun was bound to, using `RunRecord.sandboxKey` — recorded by the detach path at\nthe moment it still knew the compound key, because the reaper has none of the\ninputs (`threadId`, workspace hash, tenant, reuse strategy) needed to re-derive\nit. It answers `'destroyed'`, `'destroy-failed'`, `'no-sandbox-key'`,\n`'not-found'`, or `'provider-mismatch'`.\n\nTwo orderings are load-bearing:\n\n- **The provider check comes first**, before either `destroy` or `delete`. A\n  multi-provider app would otherwise hand a Docker container id to Daytona's\n  `destroy`, which at best errors and at worst matches an unrelated sandbox in\n  the other provider's id namespace. A mismatch therefore touches **nothing**,\n  including the instance record, which the *right* provider still needs.\n- **`destroy` before `delete`, and `delete` regardless of whether `destroy`\n  threw.** The provider sandbox may already be gone (idle-reclaimed, region\n  wiped, container pruned). Keeping an instance record that points at nothing\n  guarantees a failed `resume` on the thread's next turn — a broken user\n  experience — whereas an orphaned provider sandbox is a bounded cost the\n  provider itself reclaims. `delete` is therefore unconditional, but the\n  *outcome* must not claim success when `destroy` threw: `'destroy-failed'` is\n  reported instead of `'destroyed'`, because the instance record is gone\n  either way and an operator needs to tell \"torn down cleanly\" apart from\n  \"possibly still billing, and now unreachable from here since\n  `SandboxInstanceStore` has no `list`\". `sandboxReclaimer` logs\n  `'destroy-failed'` above debug level for exactly that reason — every other\n  outcome is bookkeeping an operator never needs to see.\n\n`sandboxReclaimer(options)` is the same thing adapted to `ReapOptions.reclaim`:\nit logs the outcome and resolves — **except on `'destroy-failed'`, where it\nrejects** with a `SandboxReclaimFailedError`. The reaper calls it **only** once\nthe record actually reached a terminal status, and with the *originally listed*\nrecord rather than the one the drive returned — a failed terminal `update`\nyields a locally rebuilt record with no `sandboxKey`, which would answer\n`'no-sandbox-key'` and leak the sandbox silently on exactly the path where\nsomething already went wrong.\n\nThat rejection is deliberate, and it is what makes the `reclaim-failed` outcome\nreachable at all. `ReapOptions.reclaim` is `(record) => Promise<void>`, so a\nrejection is the sweep's only channel for \"the sandbox was NOT reclaimed\":\n\nWire the reclaimer as shown above (`reclaim: sandboxReclaimer({ provider,\ninstances })`), then inspect the summary the sweep hands back:\n\n```ts\nimport { SandboxReclaimFailedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport type { ReapResult } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nexport function alertOnLeakedSandboxes(\n  result: ReapResult,\n  alert: (message: string, detail: Record<string, unknown>) => void,\n): void {\n  // Watch this counter — it is the leak alarm.\n  if (result.outcomes['reclaim-failed'] === 0) return\n\n  for (const run of result.runs) {\n    if (run.outcome !== 'reclaim-failed') continue\n    // The transcript IS saved and the record IS terminal; only the teardown\n    // failed. `status`/`exitCode` are reported exactly as `finalized` reports\n    // them, which is what distinguishes this from a `failed` sweep.\n    const leakedKey =\n      run.error instanceof SandboxReclaimFailedError\n        ? run.error.sandboxKey\n        : undefined\n\n    alert('sandbox may still be billing', {\n      runId: run.runId,\n      status: run.status,\n      sandboxKey: leakedKey,\n      // Present ONLY if the drive also outran `runBudgetMs`. `reclaim-failed`\n      // overwrites the `budget-exceeded` outcome, so this field is what keeps\n      // that second diagnostic on the entry.\n      budgetAnomaly: run.terminalizedAnyway !== undefined,\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA `reclaim-failed` run has already left `listReclaimable` for good, so no later\nsweep retries it — that entry is the only notice you will get. A custom\n`reclaim` should follow the same convention: reject when the sandbox was not\ntorn down, resolve when there was nothing to tear down.\n\n## Sizing `detachedRunTtlMs` and the sweep interval\n\n`detachedRunTtlMs` is a wall-clock cap, in milliseconds, on a *running agent\nwith nobody watching*. There is no default and no string parsing: it is a\nrequired, plain number on `ReapOptions`, and deliberately lives only there —\n`withSandbox` cannot enforce a TTL itself, since `reapDetachedRuns` runs from a\ncron with no chat request in flight and has no capability bus to read it\nfrom. Passing it directly to the sweep is what keeps it a single source of\ntruth instead of two settings that could silently disagree.\n\nSize it from your agent's honest p99 task duration, not from user patience: too\nshort and you cancel real work a user was going to come back to; too long and an\nabandoned run bills for that long. If a coding agent legitimately runs 20\nminutes, 30 minutes (`30 * 60 * 1000`) is tight and an hour is defensible.\n\nThen keep the sweep interval well **under** the TTL. The interval bounds how\nlong *past* the TTL an expired run survives, and it also bounds how long a run\nthat finished while detached waits before its transcript lands. A one-minute\nalarm against a 30-minute TTL costs one cheap store query per minute and makes\nboth windows negligible. Sweeping every 30 minutes against a 30-minute TTL means\nan expiry can be an hour late.\n\n`maxRuns` (default 25) and the sequential per-run loop exist so one invocation\ncannot outrun a Worker's CPU budget or a Lambda timeout and be killed mid-drive.\nIf a backlog exceeds the batch, the next tick takes the next batch — reduce the\ninterval rather than raising the cap.\n\n## Retention: three clocks, and only one of them is ours\n\nThe reaper closes runs and reclaims sandboxes. It does **not** garbage-collect\nyour stored data, and the split is deliberate:\n\n- **Event-log retention is your durability backend's job**, inside whatever\n  `StreamDurability` you wired. The framework never deletes from it, because it\n  does not own the storage — `memoryStream` evicts completed runs after a grace\n  window, `durableStream` retains per its backend's own policy, and a custom\n  adapter retains per yours. **30 days is a reasonable default**: long enough\n  that a user who reopens a thread the next morning still gets an exact replay of\n  the run, short enough that a chatty deployment does not accumulate raw chunk\n  logs forever.\n- **Message retention is the message store's job**, and it must **outlive** event\n  retention. The transcript is the durable artifact; the event log is a delivery\n  detail. Once a run's events age out, the thread must still render — see the\n  client half below.\n- **Journals are bounded inside the sandbox** by `pruneJournals`, and vanish with\n  the sandbox when `reclaimSandbox` destroys it.\n\nThe ordering is what matters: events may age out first, messages must not.\n\n## Client: tolerate an aged-out event log\n\nThe usual case needs nothing. `useChat` with `persistence: true` fetches the\ntranscript on mount and only then tails whatever run is still generating, so a\nrun whose event log is gone simply paints from messages.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Thread({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  // On mount this GETs the transcript from the message store, then tails an\n  // `activeRun` if `reconstructChat` reports one. An aged-out event log means\n  // the tail yields nothing — the transcript is already painted, so the thread\n  // still renders correctly. This is why message retention must outlive event\n  // retention.\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{messages.length} messages</p>\n      <button onClick={() => void sendMessage('Continue')}>Continue</button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you drive the rejoin yourself, make the fallback explicit: join the run, and\nif it yields nothing, fall back to the stored transcript rather than showing an\nempty thread.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ThreadView({ threadId, runId }: {\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState('joining')\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function join(): Promise<void> {\n      let received = 0\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        received += 1\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n      // Zero chunks from a run the server knew about means its delivery log\n      // aged out (or was pruned). The transcript is still authoritative.\n      if (received === 0) setStatus('replaced-by-transcript')\n      else setStatus('joined')\n    }\n\n    void join().catch(() => setStatus('replaced-by-transcript'))\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [runId])\n\n  // Reconstructed from the message store — always render this, and let the\n  // joined events refine it. Never gate the thread on the event log existing.\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>\n        {status}: {chunks.length} live events, thread {threadId}\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe same rule applies server-side: a `GET` that only replays the log will serve\nan empty stream for an aged-out run. Route reconstruct-from-messages first and\nresume second, as [Persistence Overview](../persistence/overview) shows.\n\n## The limitation: there is no instance-store `list`\n\n`SandboxInstanceStore` is `get` / `upsert` / `delete`, with no enumeration, and\nthat is a deliberate refusal: adding `list` would force every backend and the\nconformance suite to grow an enumeration for one hypothetical caller.\n\nThe consequence is real and documented rather than hidden. **A sandbox whose run\nrecord was deleted before any sweep saw it is unreachable.** Nothing can name its\nkey, so `reclaimSandbox` can never be called for it, and it survives until the\nprovider's own idle reclamation takes it. Two things keep that rare: prune run\nrecords only after their runs are terminal *and* their sandboxes reclaimed, and\nset a provider-side idle timeout as the backstop.\n\n## See also\n\n- [Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs): where this sweep fits in the whole\n  picture, in plain language and with no code\n- [Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover): how a run becomes detached in the first\n  place, `sandboxRunDriver`, and the single-writer fencing the sweep reuses\n- [The Run Journal](./journal): the journal `probeRunExit` reads and\n  `pruneJournals` bounds\n- [Store Reference](../persistence/store-reference):\n  `listReclaimable`, and the run fields a reapable backend must round-trip\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](./durability): the instance store\n  `reclaimSandbox` deletes from\n"}
